1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: Spaced evenly, the power strokes come every seven hundred and twenty degrees, divided by the number of cylinders.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: With four cylinders, that's every 180 degrees, which gives a push every half turn.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: And a straight four usually fires its cylinders in the order one, three, four, two.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: Not one-two-three-four?

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: One, three, four, two is the usual order. Some British engines used one, two, four, three.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: Here's the twisting force that the gas puts on the crank, in an idealised model.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: One cylinder gives one big shove, then a long wait, with a drag while it compresses.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: Four cylinders give one shove after another.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: With six cylinders, the power strokes overlap, and the push is smoother still.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: And even spacing means less vibration, and a smoother sound.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: There's another way to light the fire, with no spark at all.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: A diesel draws in only air, and squeezes it much harder, typically fifteen to twenty-three to one.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERB: And squeezing air that hard heats it by hundreds of degrees.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: Near the top, fuel is sprayed in, and it ignites in the hot air by itself.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERB: So the squeeze itself does the spark plug's job.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERA: In a way. With no fuel in the cylinder during the squeeze, nothing can ignite early, so it can squeeze harder.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERB: That's one reason diesels are efficient. Big, slow ship diesels reach up to 55 percent.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: And instead of throttling its air, a diesel sets its power by how much fuel it injects.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: Rudolf Diesel's first prototype fired in 1893, and his third, tested in 1897, turned about 26 percent of its fuel into work.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: And it runs the same four strokes of intake, compression, power and exhaust.
